00:10:15.330 --> 00:10:18.210
says that experts estimate that as much as

00:10:18.210 --> 00:10:22.680
90% of online content may
be synthetically generated

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:24.510
by 2026.

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:25.890
90%.

00:10:25.890 --> 00:10:27.850
Meaning AI will be
making most of the stuff

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.520
that we watch on the internet.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:30.990
Their big fear in all of this

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:32.677
is that deepfakes will, quote,

00:10:32.677 --> 00:10:34.890
"lead to a situation where citizens

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:37.470
no longer have a shared reality,"

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:38.938
causing what they call

00:10:38.938 --> 00:10:41.070
(deep voice) an information apocalypse.

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.110
(normal voice) Here is a 43 page report

00:10:43.110 --> 00:10:46.020
from the US Department
of Homeland Security,

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:47.850
and look at this title page!

00:10:47.850 --> 00:10:49.470
I mean, look at that graphic design!

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:50.730
Especially this moment,

00:10:50.730 --> 00:10:54.600
where it goes from a classic
sans serif, to a serif.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:56.430
I mean, it's not bad for the government.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:00.180
DHS says that deepfakes and
the misuse of synthetic content

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:03.690
pose a clear, present, and
evolving threat to the public,

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:06.180
across national security, law enforcement,

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.880
financial, and societal domains.

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:10.876
The Pentagon is using
its big research wing,

00:11:10.876 --> 00:11:13.170
the one that helped invent, I don't know,

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:16.680
the GPS, the COVID vaccine,
and the literal internet.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:17.513
That one.

00:11:17.513 --> 00:11:20.160
To look into deepfakes,
and how to combat them.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:22.290
Like, they're taking this very seriously.

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.720
But my big problem with
all of these reports is,

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.670
they're couched in such vague language!

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:28.830
What can deepfakes actually be used for,

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:31.920
outside of making really cool
Kendrick Lamar music videos

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:33.635
and TikTok Tom Cruises?

00:11:33.635 --> 00:11:35.503
- I think there's bubble gum inside this.

00:11:35.503 --> 00:11:36.420
(electronic whirring)

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:39.957
- Well, to get a taste, look
no further than Ukraine.

00:11:39.957 --> 00:11:42.990
- The Russian president
says a military operation

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.176
is now underway in eastern Ukraine.

00:11:45.176 --> 00:11:47.460
- [Woman] A column of Russian armor

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:51.271
crossing into Ukrainian
territory from the north.

00:11:51.271 --> 00:11:55.530
(speaks foreign language)

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:59.580
- This is not Ukrainian
president Volodymyr Zelenskyy.

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:01.110
This is a deepfaked video

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:04.380
that appeared on a Russian
language Ukrainian news site

00:12:04.380 --> 00:12:07.590
just four weeks after
Russia invaded Ukraine.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:09.660
Russian troops were
trying to take over Kiev,

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:12.330
which would've meant a
massive victory for Putin.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.850
Information on the ground
was scant and foggy,

00:12:14.850 --> 00:12:16.620
and this video pops up,

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:18.690
of Volodymyr Zelenskyy urging his troops

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:20.820
to surrender to Russian forces.

00:12:20.820 --> 00:12:23.130
Now listen, this is kind of a
crappy version of a deepfake.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:26.940
I mean, misshapen head,
weird accent and voice.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:28.440
Wasn't super pulled off.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:29.370
But guess what?

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.896
It doesn't matter, because
what this video did do

00:12:31.896 --> 00:12:34.828
is it made people
question every other video

00:12:34.828 --> 00:12:36.540
coming out of Ukraine.

00:12:36.540 --> 00:12:38.340
And this is what Congress meant

00:12:38.340 --> 00:12:40.440
when they were freaking out in 2018

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.690
about the idea of
deepfakes blurring the line

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:44.070
between fact and fiction,

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:46.710
undermining trust in
recorded images and videos

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:49.050
as objective depictions of reality.

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:51.810
And this is actually, so
far in my reporting on this,

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:53.130
the biggest takeaway.

00:12:53.130 --> 00:12:55.710
I don't know if we realize
what a big shift this is!

00:12:55.710 --> 00:12:58.110
As deepfake technology gets better,

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:01.080
yes, it allows people to create
compelling fake evidence,

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:02.490
and that is worrisome.

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.490
But it also allows people
to dismiss real evidence,

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:06.967
real footage, as,

00:13:06.967 --> 00:13:08.010
"Oh, that's just a deepfake.

00:13:08.010 --> 00:13:09.826
We can do that now. We
can't trust anything."

00:13:09.826 --> 00:13:11.670
- It's not just what they could create.

00:13:11.670 --> 00:13:15.810
It's the doubt that is cast
on authentic video and audio.

00:13:15.810 --> 00:13:16.872
- Yeah.

00:13:16.872 --> 00:13:18.270
- Okay, let's get to another
concrete threat here,

00:13:18.270 --> 00:13:19.710
to get us away from all the vague language

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:22.470
of government agencies talking
about how scary this all is.

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:23.859
Think about the legal system.

00:13:23.859 --> 00:13:27.120
(electronic whirring)

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:30.360
Deepfakes are becoming a
nightmare for evidence in court.

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:33.210
In 2020, there was a child custody case,

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:35.310
where the mother presented as evidence

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.860
audio recording of the child's father

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.620
saying violent things over the phone.

00:13:40.620 --> 00:13:42.390
It was submitted as legit evidence,

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:45.300
as proof to be like, "This
dad is unfit to have the kid."

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:47.160
But after some digital forensics,

00:13:47.160 --> 00:13:50.380
it became clear that the mother
had used an online tutorial

00:13:50.380 --> 00:13:54.180
and some cheap software
to doctor the audio file,

00:13:54.180 --> 00:13:57.480
to add in fake violent words.

00:13:57.480 --> 00:14:00.780
Most judges and juries
are not ready for this!

00:14:00.780 --> 00:14:03.420
Most wouldn't think to
question evidence like this

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:06.870
that sounds like a smoking
gun, and yet was totally fake.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:10.020
This sort of technology
is getting so accessible

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:11.460
and easy to use.

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:14.070
In fact, this VO that you're
listening to right now

00:14:14.070 --> 00:14:15.600
is actually fake.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:17.580
I made it using a cheap and easy software

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:19.320
widely available to anyone.

00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:21.060
Now imagine that you're on a jury,

00:14:21.060 --> 00:14:23.070
and you're presiding over a criminal case.

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:24.060
And even though there may not be

00:14:24.060 --> 00:14:26.100
any manipulated evidence in front of you,

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:27.630
there's now a nagging voice

00:14:27.630 --> 00:14:29.677
in the back of your mind, that's like,

00:14:29.677 --> 00:14:31.980
"Remember that time that Johnny tricked me

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:35.460
in that YouTube video, and
he made his face change,

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:37.350
and now he looks like someone else,

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:38.580
and it wasn't really him,

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:39.810
and it was really convincing?

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:41.700
Who's to say all of this visual evidence

00:14:41.700 --> 00:14:44.010
that I'm seeing in court isn't also fake?"

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:46.650
Visual evidence is no longer rock solid.

00:14:46.650 --> 00:14:49.920
Surveillance footage, body cam footage,

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:52.560
heck, even audio taken in a bus

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:53.970
from a presidential candidate.

00:14:53.970 --> 00:14:56.160
This was all solid at one point,

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:58.452
and now it can all be
called into question.

00:14:58.452 --> 00:14:59.730
(electronic whirring)

00:14:59.730 --> 00:15:00.563
And then of course,

00:15:00.563 --> 00:15:02.250
deepfakes are being used for

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.170
good old fashioned cyber crime.

00:15:04.170 --> 00:15:05.070
Man, cyber crime!

00:15:05.070 --> 00:15:06.573
It just sounds so quaint.

00:15:07.470 --> 00:15:08.940
Cyber crime.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:10.470
Elon Musk was recently faked

00:15:10.470 --> 00:15:12.233
to help shill a new crypto scam.

00:15:12.233 --> 00:15:15.180
- (indistinct), a site that
will help ordinary people

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:16.500
to gain financial independence.

00:15:16.500 --> 00:15:19.500
- And yeah, this one's
obviously fake and poorly done.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:20.970
My point is they're getting better,

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:23.760
and some scams are already
way more persuasive.

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:25.080
Like this group of fraudsters

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:28.140
who were able to clone the
voice of a major bank director,

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:32.707
and then use it to steal $35
million in cold hard cash,

00:15:32.707 --> 00:15:33.903
$35 million!

00:15:34.787 --> 00:15:36.030
- [Man] That's a lot of money.

00:15:36.030 --> 00:15:37.890
- Just by deepfaking this guy's voice,

00:15:37.890 --> 00:15:39.510
and like, using it to make a phone call

00:15:39.510 --> 00:15:41.070
to transfer a bunch of money.

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:41.913
And it worked!

00:15:43.229 --> 00:15:46.110
Okay, but in reality,

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:48.570
the risk of eroding trust in the public

00:15:48.570 --> 00:15:50.160
or weakening our legal system

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.770
or giving cyber criminals a new weapon,

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:56.790
all of these are actually
the rarest uses of deepfakes.

00:15:56.790 --> 00:15:59.340
The main victims of this new technology,

00:15:59.340 --> 00:16:01.410
at least at the moment, are women.

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:04.500
- [Man] These sexually suggestive
ads are popping up online.

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:06.390
- Sexual misuse of these deepfakes.

00:16:06.390 --> 00:16:08.040
- She's been deepfaked.

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:10.050
- [Woman] That's not me.

00:16:10.050 --> 00:16:13.770
- By one estimate, 96%
of deepfake production

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:15.660
is used to produce porn.

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:17.640
Almost all of which are using women

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:19.410
who have not consented to this.

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:21.570
Just two years ago,
Telegram got in trouble,

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:23.220
because it was found that they had

00:16:23.220 --> 00:16:25.170
private groups on their service

00:16:25.170 --> 00:16:26.946
that were using deepfake technology

00:16:26.946 --> 00:16:30.020
to remove the clothes of more
than a hundred thousand women.

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.370
It used to just be the
faces of celebrities,

00:16:32.370 --> 00:16:34.350
because they're the ones who have all this

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:36.090
high quality data out in the world

00:16:36.090 --> 00:16:37.860
that you can train a model on.

00:16:37.860 --> 00:16:39.060
But no longer.

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:40.560
The tech has developed such that

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:43.440
an ordinary person with a
few images on social media

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:45.330
could suddenly have their faces deepfaked

00:16:45.330 --> 00:16:47.010
onto the body of a porn star.

00:16:47.010 --> 00:16:48.720
- [Man] Martin has found dozens

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:51.660
of doctored images and videos of herself.

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:54.813
She has no idea who's
responsible for them.

00:16:55.650 --> 00:16:57.780
- Okay, but what's being done about this?

00:16:57.780 --> 00:16:58.980
What can be done?

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:01.165
Let's first remember that when
a new technology comes along,

00:17:01.165 --> 00:17:04.020
it typically evolves rapidly,

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:05.760
way faster than the lawmakers

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:08.280
who need to understand
it to regulate it do.

00:17:08.280 --> 00:17:10.980
So, we're in the kind of wild west phase,

00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:12.840
where the lawmakers
are kind of just trying

00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:13.673
to get their head around this stuff.

00:17:13.673 --> 00:17:16.530
- Let's hope that Congress can catch up.

00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:18.810
- China is actually the first
country to have regulated

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:21.180
what they call deep
synthesis technologies,

00:17:21.180 --> 00:17:23.700
by requiring all deepfake content

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:26.490
to be clearly marked as
having been modified.

00:17:26.490 --> 00:17:28.920
Luckily, a surveillance
state is a lot easier

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:30.660
to regulate this kind of stuff.

00:17:30.660 --> 00:17:32.910
But the EU is trying.

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.400
- [Woman] In an ambitious
bill called the AI Act.

00:17:35.400 --> 00:17:36.660
- Boy do I love going through

00:17:36.660 --> 00:17:39.239
European Commission PowerPoint slides.

00:17:39.239 --> 00:17:41.730
Like China, the EU is trying to make it

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:44.310
so that you have to label deepfakes.

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:45.750
But of course, in a Western democracy,

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:46.897
you have to put this line,

00:17:46.897 --> 00:17:48.960
"unless necessary for the exercise

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.700
of a fundamental right or freedom,

00:17:50.700 --> 00:17:52.440
or for reasons of public interest."

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:55.440
Oh man, those pesky rights
always getting in the way

00:17:55.440 --> 00:17:56.820
of being able to regulate stuff.

00:17:56.820 --> 00:17:58.833
The UK is also trying to figure this out.

00:17:58.833 --> 00:18:01.680
They are targeting the porn situation,

00:18:01.680 --> 00:18:04.260
the one that is the mass majority
of the uses of deepfakes.

00:18:04.260 --> 00:18:06.660
Doing deepfake porn without consent

00:18:06.660 --> 00:18:08.520
comes with a penalty of prison,

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:11.370
though it's super hard
to enforce this stuff.

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:12.360
That's what's so tricky.

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:13.530
But they're trying.

00:18:13.530 --> 00:18:15.480
- Will the government take urgent action

00:18:15.480 --> 00:18:16.950
and repair this mess?

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:19.980
- And then there's tech
companies, like here on YouTube.

00:18:19.980 --> 00:18:23.227
They say that they will pull
down any video that, quote,

00:18:23.227 --> 00:18:27.030
"Includes deceptive uses
of manipulated media,

00:18:27.030 --> 00:18:30.690
ie. deepfakes, which may
pose serious risk of harm."

00:18:30.690 --> 00:18:32.460
Who decides what serious risk of harm is?

00:18:32.460 --> 00:18:33.780
Meh.

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:35.640
But one promising solution here

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:38.370
would be to fight software with software.

00:18:38.370 --> 00:18:39.720
If we could train software

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:42.150
to detect the quirks of deepfakes,

00:18:42.150 --> 00:18:43.920
like the unnatural blinking,

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:45.810
or some of the other weird subtle things

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:47.460
that the human eye can't pick up on,

00:18:47.460 --> 00:18:49.320
we would have a strong way of verifying

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:52.050
what is real footage,
and what is distorted.

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:55.830
So deepfakes are here, and
they're seemingly here to stay.

00:18:55.830 --> 00:18:57.750
We are entering a new chapter

00:18:57.750 --> 00:19:01.290
of how humans consume and
relate to information.

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:03.660
It's a chapter as significant in my mind

00:19:03.660 --> 00:19:05.910
as some of the other big chapters,

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:08.940
like when we learned to
capture light and record it.

00:19:08.940 --> 00:19:10.530
It changed the world!

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:14.040
Photographs could be
used in court as proof.

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:15.960
They could be used in science to capture

00:19:15.960 --> 00:19:18.060
and understand the world around us.

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:21.453
In journalism, it allowed
us to show, not just tell.

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:24.600
And when we made those images move,

00:19:24.600 --> 00:19:26.610
we could capture more truth,

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:29.010
more proof, more evidence.

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:30.510
And then we could connect,

00:19:30.510 --> 00:19:33.060
and spread those images far and wide.

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:36.990
Truth and evidence coursed
through the world's internet.

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:39.390
This evolution of the moving image

00:19:39.390 --> 00:19:41.970
and its uses in the connected world

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:44.070
is taking an unexpected turn,

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:46.680
away from being the bedrock of evidence,

00:19:46.680 --> 00:19:50.460
and towards the foggy territory
of deception and confusion.

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:53.430
We're gonna have to navigate
this chapter carefully.

00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:55.020
We're gonna have to push even harder

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:57.510
to know where our images came from,

00:19:57.510 --> 00:19:59.850
who made them, and for what purpose.

00:19:59.850 --> 00:20:01.770
And we're gonna have to resist the urge

00:20:01.770 --> 00:20:06.607
to believe everything we see,
no matter how real it looks.

00:20:06.607 --> 00:20:09.190
(solemn music)

